TP.register({id:"lp10-g5",lp:10,grade:"5",mode:"curriculum",checks:9,nextGrade:"6th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FLU_TITLE="The Year in Numbers";const FLU=["Every June the hallway outside the office at Harbor Crest turns into a wall of numbers.","The idea belongs to Ms. Adeyemi, who started it eight years ago with a single sheet of poster board and a marker. It has grown. The wall now takes up forty feet of hallway, and the fifth grade spends most of a week putting it together.","The rule is that every number on the wall has to be one somebody actually counted. Nothing is rounded up to look better, and nothing goes up without the name of the person who counted it written small in the corner. One year a number appeared with no name on it, and Ms. Adeyemi took it down herself and left the gap there until somebody owned it.","What ends up on the wall is never quite what the fifth grade expects. Books read, yes, and miles run at field day. But also: 1,140 lunches served on the busiest day, 26 pairs of lost gloves and 41 singles that nobody ever claimed, and one broken stapler, replaced in October, still working in June."].join("\n\n");const GLOVE_TITLE="Twenty-Six Pairs";const GLOVE=["Counting the lost and found was the job nobody picked, so Delphine took it, which is how she ended up on the floor of the back closet on a Thursday afternoon with the door propped open and the light on.","There were gloves everywhere. She sorted them into pairs first, then into singles, and then she sat back on her heels and looked at what she had.","Twenty-six pairs. Forty-one singles.","She wrote both numbers down. Then she crossed out the second one.","On Friday she put it back. When Ms. Adeyemi asked her why, Delphine said that a single glove was still something somebody lost, and that leaving it off the wall made the year look tidier than it really was.","Ms. Adeyemi did not say anything for a moment. Then she found a marker and wrote Delphine's name in the corner, small, the way the rule said."].join("\n\n");const WALL_TITLE="Leave the Gloves";const WALL=["By Wednesday the fifth grade had hit a wall.","They had been measuring and cutting and taping since Monday, and the strip outside the office was still only half covered. Ravi said the project had turned into a hungry animal: you fed it a whole afternoon and it asked for another one.","Ms. Adeyemi came out at two o'clock, looked at the gap, and said, “Leave the gloves. Do the books.”","It was like somebody opened a window. The books column was already counted, already checked, and already in order. By three the strip was moving again, and the gloves waited until Thursday without anybody minding.","Later Ravi said the trick was not working harder. The trick was knowing which part of the wall was ready to go up."].join("\n\n");const COUNT_TITLE="How to Count Something Big";const COUNT=["Nobody counts a crowd by counting people one at a time. Past a few dozen, the count drifts: people move, you lose your place, and two careful counters end up with two different answers.","What counters do instead is divide. A field is split into squares with tape or cones. One person counts one square, writes the number down, and moves on. At the end the squares are added. The method is slower to set up and far more accurate, because a mistake inside one square changes one number instead of all of them.","For things that stay still, such as books on a shelf, there is a second method. You count one shelf carefully, measure how long that shelf is, and multiply by the number of shelves the same length. This is called sampling. It is fast, and it is only as good as the shelf you picked, so good counters pick several shelves and compare them.","The rule both methods share is that a count has to be written down the moment it is made. A number carried in your head across a room is not a count. It is a guess that sounds like one."].join("\n\n");const S1={title:"Counting by Squares",src:"a chapter from the book <i>Field Methods for Young Scientists</i> by Nadia Olsen, 2022",body:"Split the area into equal squares, count one square at a time, and write each number down before you move to the next. Two counters working the same grid should land within a few percent of each other. If they do not, the squares are too big and should be made smaller."};const S2={title:"How Libraries Estimate a Collection",src:"a page on the Statewide Library Service website by Hugo Breen, 2024",body:"A full count of a large collection takes weeks of staff time. Most libraries sample instead. They count several shelves, average the result, and multiply by the number of shelves. The method lands within about five percent of the true figure when at least ten shelves are sampled."};const WALL_READ=["What the Wall Showed This Year.","Harbor Crest's wall of numbers went up on the last Friday of the year, all forty feet of it, and three things stood out.","First, reading. Students logged 9,400 books over the year, up from 8,100 the year before.","Second, the garden. The fifth-grade beds produced 212 pounds of vegetables, almost all of it in April and May, and the kitchen used every pound of it in school lunches.","Third, and the one nobody expected, attendance. The school's average daily attendance was the highest it has been in nine years, and the biggest rise was on Mondays."].join("\n\n");const WORDS=["accurate","estimate","inventory","responsible","significant","distribution","unreliable","circumference","preliminary","interrupted","transparent","colleague"];const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const group=(inner,label)=>`<div style="text-align:center">${inner}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:4px 0 0">${label}</p></div>`;const lookBack=(label,inner)=>`<details class="look-back" open><summary>Look back at ${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`;const pills=(...w)=>`<div class="row center" style="margin-top:18px;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap">${w.map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.3rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`;const twoSources=()=>`<div class="texts">${[S1,S2].map((t,i)=>`<article><h3>Source ${i+1}: ${t.title}</h3><p class="small" style="margin:0 0 6px">${t.src}</p><p>${t.body}</p></article>`).join("")}</div>`;const countHtml=small=>F.passage(COUNT,COUNT_TITLE,!!small);function areaModel(cols,rows,sc,sr,wLab,hLab){const SIDE=216,cw=SIDE/cols,ch=SIDE/rows,ox=96,oy=58,GW=SIDE,GH=SIDE,W=ox+GW+76,H=oy+GH+46;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="A square one yard on each side, cut into ${cols} columns and ${rows} rows. The block ${sc} columns wide and ${sr} rows tall in the top left corner is shaded.">`;for(let r=0;r<rows;r++)for(let c=0;c<cols;c++)s+=`<rect x="${ox+c*cw}" y="${oy+r*ch}" width="${cw}" height="${ch}" fill="${c<sc&&r<sr?"var(--gold)":"var(--surface)"}" fill-opacity="${c<sc&&r<sr?".65":"1"}" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<rect x="${ox}" y="${oy}" width="${GW}" height="${GH}" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3.5"/>`;s+=`<path d="M${ox} ${oy-8} v-10 h${sc*cw} v10" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${ox+sc*cw/2}" y="${oy-26}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${wLab}</text>`;s+=`<path d="M${ox-8} ${oy} h-10 v${sr*ch} h10" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;s+=`<text x="${ox-26}" y="${oy+sr*ch/2}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${hLab}</text>`;s+=`<text x="${ox+GW/2}" y="${oy+GH+32}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">1 yard</text>`;s+=`<text x="${ox+GW+10}" y="${oy+GH/2}" font-size="18" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">1 yard</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}function bars(o){const n=o.cats.length,rows=Math.round(o.max/o.step),cell=36,x0=92,y0=34,bw=152,W=x0+n*bw+24,H=y0+rows*cell+58;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W} ${H}" width="${W}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="Bar graph: ${o.title}">`;s+=`<text x="${x0+n*bw/2}" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;for(let r=0;r<=rows;r++){const y=y0+(rows-r)*cell;s+=`<line x1="${x0}" y1="${y}" x2="${x0+n*bw}" y2="${y}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${x0-10}" y="${y}" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" font-size="16" fill="var(--ink)">${(r*o.step).toLocaleString()}</text>`}o.cats.forEach((c,i)=>{const h=o.vals[i]/o.max*rows*cell,bx=x0+i*bw+bw*.2;s+=`<rect x="${bx}" y="${y0+rows*cell-h}" width="${bw*.6}" height="${h}" fill="var(--sea)" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="2"/>`;s+=`<text x="${bx+bw*.3}" y="${y0+rows*cell+26}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">${c}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}const BOOKS_GRAPH=()=>bars({title:"Books logged at Harbor Crest",cats:["Two years ago","Last year","This year"],vals:[7400,8100,9400],max:1e4,step:2e3});const NUMBERS=()=>F.table(["Number on the wall","What it counts"],[["9,400","books logged by students this year"],["212","pounds of vegetables from the fifth-grade beds"],["1,140","lunches served on the busiest day"],["26 pairs and 41 singles","lost gloves nobody claimed"],["40","feet of hallway the wall covers"]],{caption:"Five of this year's numbers"});const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Read the Wall",short:"Read Aloud",parts:[{std:["RF.5.4"],kind:"read",n:0,label:"words read correctly",dur:100,dir:"Read the text out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a grade-level text read aloud",html:()=>F.passage(FLU,FLU_TITLE),key:["__WC__ words, not counting the title. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy. Let the student finish the passage.","Spring grade 5 benchmark: about 146 words correct per minute (Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017, spring 50th percentile). 25th percentile is about 119. This is the last check of the year, so the spring column is the right one.","Note pace, phrasing, and expression. Listen for the list in the last paragraph read as a list, with a small drop at each comma, and for the numbers read smoothly rather than spelled out digit by digit."]}]},{id:2,title:"Share It Out",short:"Sharing",parts:[{std:["5.NF.B.3"],dur:115,dir:"Answer out loud and show your work on your paper. Say what the fraction means in each answer.",desc:"interpreting a fraction as division and solving sharing problems with fraction or mixed number answers",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Everything that comes out of the garden gets shared out evenly. The shares almost never come out as whole numbers.</p>`,qs:["Write 7 ÷ 8 as a fraction. What does that fraction mean?","Five students share 3 granola bars equally. How much does each student get?","Four people share 9 feet of ribbon equally. How much ribbon does each person get? Give a mixed number.","Write 23/5 as a mixed number.","212 pounds of vegetables are packed equally into 8 crates. How many pounds go in each crate?","Three trays are shared among four people. Without working it out exactly, is each share more or less than one whole tray? How do you know?"],key:["7/8. A fraction is a division: 7/8 means 7 wholes shared equally among 8, so each share is 7/8 of a whole. Partial: 7/8 right with no account of what it means is approaching. (5.NF.B.3)","3/5 of a bar each. 3 ÷ 5 = 3/5. Partial: 5/3 reverses the share and does not count. (5.NF.B.3)","2 1/4 feet each. 9 ÷ 4 = 9/4, and 9/4 = 2 1/4. Partial: 9/4 right with no mixed number given is approaching. (5.NF.B.3, a mixed number answer)","4 3/5. 23 ÷ 5 is 4 with 3 left over, and the 3 left over is 3/5 of the next whole. Partial: 4 remainder 3 is approaching, since the leftover has to be named as a fraction. (5.NF.B.3)","26 1/2 pounds in each crate. 212 ÷ 8 = 26 remainder 4, and 4/8 is 1/2, so each crate holds 26 1/2 pounds. Accept 26.5. Partial: 26 remainder 4 is approaching, since the leftover has to be shared too. (5.NF.B.3)","Less than one whole tray. There are fewer trays than people, so 3 ÷ 4 = 3/4, and 3/4 is less than 1. Partial: saying less with no reason from the numbers is approaching. (5.NF.B.3, reasoning about the size of the answer)"]}]},{id:3,title:"Counting Words",short:"Word Cards",parts:[{std:["RF.5.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:70,dir:"Read each word out loud. Break it into chunks you already know before you say the whole word.",desc:"decoding unfamiliar multisyllabic words using syllable patterns, roots, and affixes",key:["Read each word in order: accurate, estimate, inventory, responsible, significant, distribution, unreliable, circumference, preliminary, interrupted, transparent, colleague.","Roots and affixes to listen for: circum- means around and trans- means across (circumference, transparent), inter- means between (interrupted), pre- means before (preliminary), dis- means apart (distribution), un- means not (unreliable), and the endings -ate, -ory, -ible, -ant, -ion, -ary.","Syllable breaks: ac-cu-rate, es-ti-mate, in-ven-to-ry, re-spon-si-ble, sig-nif-i-cant, dis-tri-bu-tion, un-re-li-a-ble, cir-cum-fer-ence, pre-lim-i-nar-y, in-ter-rupt-ed, trans-par-ent, col-league.","colleague is borrowed from French: the -league ending sounds like leeg, with the ue silent. Count it correct if the student gets there by chunking even if the first try is off."]}]},{id:4,title:"Twenty-Six Pairs",short:"The Gloves",parts:[{std:["RL.5.1"],dur:105,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the story for each answer.",desc:"quoting accurately from a story to explain what it says and to support inferences",html:()=>F.passage(GLOVE,GLOVE_TITLE,true),qs:["Which job did Delphine take, and why was it free? Read me the exact words.","What two numbers did she end up with?","The story never says Delphine changed her mind. Read me the exact words that show she did.","Why did she put the second number back on the list? Read me the sentence that says it.","The story never says Ms. Adeyemi agreed with her. Read me the exact words that show she did."],key:["The lost and found. Quotes “Counting the lost and found was the job nobody picked, so Delphine took it.” (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)","Twenty-six pairs and forty-one singles. (RL.5.1, explicit information)","Quotes “She wrote both numbers down. Then she crossed out the second one,” and “On Friday she put it back.” Crossing it out and then putting it back is the change. (RL.5.1, quoting to support an inference)","Quotes “a single glove was still something somebody lost, and that leaving it off the wall made the year look tidier than it really was.” (RL.5.1)","Quotes “Then she found a marker and wrote Delphine's name in the corner, small, the way the rule said.” Writing the counter's name is what the wall's rule does for a number that is going up, so putting Delphine's name on it accepts her number. (RL.5.1, quoting to support an inference)"]}]},{id:5,title:"Where on the Grid",short:"Grid",parts:[{std:["5.G.A.1"],dur:115,dir:"Look at the grid. Answer out loud. Say the numbers in the right order every time.",desc:"using a pair of perpendicular axes to name points, and explaining what each coordinate means",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew maps the hallway on a grid before it tapes anything up. The corner by the office door is the origin, and one grid step is one foot.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.coordGrid({max:10,size:400,points:[[3,7,"A"],[7,3,"B"],[0,5,"C"],[6,6,"D"]],xLabel:"x",yLabel:"y"}),"The hallway grid, in feet from the office door"]),qs:["Name the ordered pair for point A.","Name the ordered pair for point B.","Points A and B use the same two numbers. Why are they not the same point?","Name the ordered pair for point C. What is special about where it sits?","Start at (0, 0) and describe exactly how to travel to point D, naming the axis you move along each time.","What is the point (0, 0) called, and why does every pair of coordinates get measured from it?"],key:["(3, 7). Three steps along the x-axis, then seven up the y-axis. Partial: (7, 3) reverses the pair and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","(7, 3). Seven steps along the x-axis, then three up the y-axis. Partial: (3, 7) reverses the pair and is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Because the order tells you which axis each number belongs to. The first number is the distance traveled from the origin along the x-axis and the second is the distance along the y-axis, so (3, 7) and (7, 3) land in different places. Partial: saying the order matters with no account of which axis each number belongs to is approaching. (5.G.A.1, the coordinates correspond in order to the two axes)","(0, 5). It sits right on the y-axis, because its x-coordinate is 0, so you travel no distance at all along the x-axis before going up. Partial: the pair right with nothing said about the y-axis is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","Travel 6 feet from the origin along the x-axis, then 6 feet up along the y-axis. That is the point (6, 6). Partial: the route right with no axis named is approaching. (5.G.A.1)","The origin. It is the point where the two perpendicular axes cross, and both distances in an ordered pair are measured from there. Partial: naming the origin with nothing said about measuring from it is approaching. (5.G.A.1)"]}]},{id:6,title:"How to Count Something Big",short:"Counting",parts:[{std:["RI.5.1"],dur:105,dir:"Read the article. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words from the article for each answer.",desc:"quoting accurately from an informational text to explain it and to support inferences",html:()=>countHtml(true),qs:["Why does counting a crowd one person at a time go wrong? Read me the exact words.","What do counters do instead? Read me the sentence that names the method.","What is sampling, and what is its weakness? Read me the words that tell you.","What rule do both methods share? Read me the sentence.","The article never says the square method takes longer at the start but saves time later. Use the article to explain why that would be true."],key:["Quotes “Past a few dozen, the count drifts: people move, you lose your place, and two careful counters end up with two different answers.” (RI.5.1, quoting accurately)","Quotes “What counters do instead is divide,” together with “A field is split into squares with tape or cones.” (RI.5.1)","Quotes “You count one shelf carefully, measure how long that shelf is, and multiply by the number of shelves the same length,” and “it is only as good as the shelf you picked.” (RI.5.1)","Quotes “a count has to be written down the moment it is made.” (RI.5.1)","The article says the square method is “slower to set up” but that “a mistake inside one square changes one number instead of all of them,” so a wrong count means recounting one square rather than starting the whole crowd again. The article never says this, so the student has to put the two details together. (RI.5.1, inference supported by quotations)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Covering the Strip",short:"Add It Up",parts:[{std:["5.NF.A.2"],dur:115,dir:"Answer out loud. Estimate first, then work it out on your paper and check that your answer is close to your estimate.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions with unlike denominators in word problems, and using benchmarks to judge whether an answer is reasonable",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The crew keeps track of how much of the forty-foot strip is covered. Every fraction below is a fraction of the same strip.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="gap:26px;align-items:flex-end;flex-wrap:wrap">${group(F.fracBar(2,1,{width:300}),"Wednesday, 1/2 covered")}${group(F.fracBar(4,3,{width:300}),"Thursday, 3/4 covered")}</div>`,qs:["On Wednesday the strip was 1/2 covered. By Thursday it was 3/4 covered. How much of the strip got covered on Thursday?","Ravi taped 2/5 of one column and Delphine taped 1/3 of the same column. How much of that column did they tape between them?","Before you work it out: is 3/8 + 2/5 more or less than 1? How do you know?","A roll held 5/6 of a yard of tape. 1/4 of a yard was used. How much is left?","Noelle says 1/2 + 1/3 = 2/5. Use 1/2 as a benchmark to show that her answer cannot be right, then give the correct answer.","Two garden beds filled 3/4 of a crate and 5/8 of a crate. Together, is that more than one full crate? By how much?"],key:["1/4 of the strip. 3/4 − 1/2 = 3/4 − 2/4 = 1/4. Partial: 1/4 right with no common denominator shown is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)","11/15 of the column. In fifteenths, 2/5 is 6/15 and 1/3 is 5/15, and 6/15 + 5/15 = 11/15. Or: thirtieths work too, 12/30 + 10/30 = 22/30 = 11/15. Partial: 3/8 comes from adding across and does not count. (5.NF.A.2)","Less than 1. Both fractions are less than 1/2, because 3/8 is less than 4/8 and 2/5 is less than 2.5/5, so the two together cannot reach a whole. Exactly, 15/40 + 16/40 = 31/40. Partial: saying less with no benchmark used is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, using benchmarks to estimate)","7/12 of a yard. In twelfths, 5/6 is 10/12 and 1/4 is 3/12, and 10/12 − 3/12 = 7/12. Partial: subtracting across, numerator from numerator and denominator from denominator, does not count. (5.NF.A.2)","She cannot be right, because 2/5 is less than 1/2 and the answer has to be more than 1/2: 1/2 is already one of the two numbers being added, and 1/3 adds more on top of it. The correct answer is 5/6, from 3/6 + 2/6. Partial: showing she is wrong with no correct answer given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2, assessing reasonableness with a benchmark)","Yes, by 3/8 of a crate. In eighths, 3/4 is 6/8, and 6/8 + 5/8 = 11/8, which is 1 3/8. That is 3/8 of a crate more than one full crate. Partial: saying yes with no amount over one crate given is approaching. (5.NF.A.2)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Leave the Gloves",short:"Word Work",parts:[{std:["RL.5.4"],dur:100,dir:"Read the story. Then answer out loud. Use the story to explain each answer.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases in a story, including figurative language such as metaphors and similes",html:()=>F.passage(WALL,WALL_TITLE,true),qs:["“By Wednesday the fifth grade had hit a wall.” Did they walk into a wall? What does the phrase mean?","The word wall is used in two different ways in this story. Point out one place where it means a real wall, and say how you can tell.","Ravi says the project “had turned into a hungry animal: you fed it a whole afternoon and it asked for another one.” What kind of comparison is that, and what does it say about the project?","“It was like somebody opened a window.” What kind of comparison is this one, and what does it tell you about how the room felt?","“By three the strip was moving again.” A strip of paper cannot move by itself. What does moving mean here?"],key:["No. To hit a wall means to get stuck and stop making progress after working hard for a while. The story shows it: they had worked since Monday and the strip was still only half covered. (RL.5.4, figurative language)","Any of: “the strip outside the office,” “looked at the gap,” “which part of the wall was ready to go up.” In those places the wall is the real hallway wall they are covering, because it is something you can look at, measure, and tape things onto. In “hit a wall” nothing is really there, so it must be figurative. (RL.5.4, the same word used literally and figuratively)","A metaphor, because it says the project was a hungry animal rather than saying it was like one. It means the project kept using up whole afternoons and still was not finished, so no amount of work made it full. (RL.5.4, metaphor)","A simile, because of the word like. It means the stuck, airless feeling lifted all at once and the work suddenly felt easy again. (RL.5.4, simile)","It means the work was making progress again, so the covered part of the strip kept growing. (RL.5.4, meaning of a word as used in the text)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Cubes in a Crate",short:"Volume",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.3"],dur:100,dir:"Look at the solid. Answer out loud. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"understanding volume as the number of unit cubes that fill a solid, with no gaps and no overlaps",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The garden crates are packed with cube-shaped boxes. Every box is 1 centimeter on each edge.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.prism(4,3,2,{unit:34}),"A solid 4 cubes long, 3 cubes deep, and 2 cubes tall"]),qs:["How many cubes are in the solid? What is its volume?","What is a unit cube, and what is its volume?","A different solid is packed with 45 unit cubes, with no gaps and no overlaps. What is its volume?","Which measures volume and which measures area: square feet or cubic feet?","One crate holds 18 unit cubes and another holds 24. Which has more volume, and how do you know?","Can two solids of different shapes have the same volume? Give an example with numbers."],key:["24 cubes, so the volume is 24 cubic centimeters. The solid is 4 by 3 by 2, and 4 × 3 × 2 = 24. Or: count one layer of 12 and double it. Partial: 24 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","A unit cube is a cube that is 1 unit along every edge, and its volume is 1 cubic unit. It is the thing volume is counted in. Partial: defining the cube with no volume given is approaching. (5.MD.C.3a)","45 cubic units. A solid packed with n unit cubes with no gaps and no overlaps has a volume of n cubic units. Partial: 45 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)","Cubic feet measures volume. Square feet measures area, which is flat and counts squares instead of cubes. Partial: matching one of the two is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)","The one that holds 24. Volume is measured by how many unit cubes fit inside with no gaps, so more cubes means more volume. Partial: naming the right one with no reason is approaching. (5.MD.C.3b)","Yes. For example a solid 2 by 2 by 6 and a solid 3 by 4 by 2 both hold 24 unit cubes, so both have a volume of 24 cubic units even though they are different shapes. Partial: saying yes with no example in numbers is approaching. (5.MD.C.3)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Fix the Wall Notes",short:"Fix It",parts:[{title:"One Mistake Each",std:["L.5.1"],dur:60,dir:"Each line from the crew's notes has one mistake. Say the whole line out loud the right way.",desc:"perfect verb tenses, shifts in tense, and correlative conjunctions",qs:["By June the fifth grade have counted every number twice.","Neither the tape or the marker was in the box.","Last Monday Ravi measured the wall and tapes the strips.","Ms. Adeyemi will has finished the list by Friday.","Both the fifth grade as well as the fourth grade helped."],key:["By June the fifth grade had counted every number twice. (L.5.1b, past perfect verb tense)","Neither the tape nor the marker was in the box. Neither goes with nor. (L.5.1e, correlative conjunctions)","Last Monday Ravi measured the wall and taped the strips. Both verbs are in the past. (L.5.1d, correcting a shift in verb tense)","Ms. Adeyemi will have finished the list by Friday. (L.5.1b, future perfect verb tense)","Both the fifth grade and the fourth grade helped. Both goes with and. (L.5.1e, correlative conjunctions)"]},{title:"Name the Part",std:["L.5.1"],dur:40,dir:"Answer out loud. Say what the word does in the sentence, not just what it is.",desc:"explaining the function of prepositions, conjunctions, and interjections, and using the present perfect",qs:["Name a preposition in this sentence and say what job it does: Delphine sat on the floor of the closet.","Name the conjunction in this sentence and say what it does: The strip was half covered. So the crew kept going.","Which word is the interjection, and what does it do? Wow, that column took all afternoon.","Say this sentence so that it tells about something finished before now: The crew counts the books."],key:["on, or of. A preposition shows how a noun is related to the rest of the sentence. Here on tells where she sat and of tells which floor. (L.5.1a, function of prepositions)","so. A conjunction joins parts of a sentence, and so joins two complete thoughts and shows that the second one is the result of the first. (L.5.1a, function of conjunctions)","Wow. An interjection shows a sudden feeling and stands apart from the rest of the sentence, so the sentence still works without it. (L.5.1a, function of interjections)","The crew has counted the books. (L.5.1b, present perfect verb tense)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Pack the Crates",short:"Cubic Units",parts:[{std:["5.MD.C.5"],dur:115,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",desc:"finding volume with the formulas for a box and adding the volumes of two boxes that do not overlap",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The garden crew packs the harvest into boxes and stacks them in the shed. Every measurement below is in whole units.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([F.prism(5,4,3,{unit:30}),"A crate 5 by 4 by 3"]),qs:["A crate is 5 feet long, 4 feet wide, and 3 feet tall. What is its volume?","A box has a base with an area of 18 square inches and a height of 7 inches. What is its volume?","A box has a volume of 120 cubic centimeters. Its base is 6 centimeters by 5 centimeters. How tall is it?","A storage bench is made of two boxes that do not overlap. One is 6 by 3 by 2 feet and the other is 4 by 3 by 2 feet. What is the total volume?","Box P is 8 by 2 by 3 inches. Box Q is 4 by 4 by 3 inches. Which has the greater volume?","How many cubes 1 foot on each edge fit inside a crate that is 3 feet by 3 feet by 3 feet?"],key:["60 cubic feet. V = l × w × h = 5 × 4 × 3 = 60. Or: 5 × 4 = 20 for the base, then 20 × 3. (5.MD.C.5a)","126 cubic inches. V = B × h = 18 × 7 = 126, where B is the area of the base. Partial: 126 with no unit named is approaching. (5.MD.C.5b)","4 centimeters. The base area is 6 × 5 = 30 square centimeters, and 120 ÷ 30 = 4. Or: 120 ÷ 6 = 20, then 20 ÷ 5 = 4. (5.MD.C.5b, an unknown edge)","60 cubic feet. The first box is 6 × 3 × 2 = 36 and the second is 4 × 3 × 2 = 24, and 36 + 24 = 60. Volumes add when the two boxes do not overlap. Or: the two together make one 10 by 3 by 2 box, 60. Partial: one of the two boxes worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5c)","They are equal. Box P is 8 × 2 × 3 = 48 cubic inches and Box Q is 4 × 4 × 3 = 48 cubic inches. Partial: naming them equal with only one volume worked out is approaching. (5.MD.C.5a)","27 cubes. 3 × 3 × 3 = 27. Or: 3 × 3 = 9 in one layer, then 3 layers. (5.MD.C.5a)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Say the Whole Thing Short",short:"Summarize",parts:[{std:["SL.5.2"],n:5,dur:105,dir:"Listen while your teacher reads. Then answer out loud. Keep each summary short and use your own words.",desc:"summarizing a text read aloud and summarizing information shown on a graph",read:WALL_READ,html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Your teacher will read you a short report about this year's wall of numbers. Listen for the three things it says stood out. Then look at the graph.</p>`,fig:()=>F.row([BOOKS_GRAPH(),"Books logged at Harbor Crest over three years"]),qs:["Summarize the whole report in two or three sentences.","Summarize just the part about the garden.","Which part of the report surprised the people who made the wall, and what did it say?","Now summarize what the graph shows, in one or two sentences.","One number from the report also appears on the graph. Which one, and do the two agree?"],key:["A short summary in the student's own words covering the three points: reading went up, the garden produced a large harvest that the kitchen used, and attendance was the highest in nine years. A retelling that repeats the whole report word for word does not count. (SL.5.2, summarizing a text read aloud)","Summary: the fifth-grade beds grew 212 pounds of vegetables, nearly all of it in April and May, and all of it went into school lunches. (SL.5.2)","Attendance. It was the highest average daily attendance in nine years, and the biggest rise came on Mondays. (SL.5.2, a detail from a text read aloud)","Summary: the number of books logged went up in each of the three years, from 7,400 to 8,100 to 9,400, so reading rose steadily. (SL.5.2, information presented in a different format)","The books figure. The report said 9,400 books this year, up from 8,100 last year, and the graph shows the same two numbers, so they agree. The graph adds a third year, 7,400, that the report did not mention. (SL.5.2)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Two Sources on Counting",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.5.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:105,dir:"Read both sources. Write your answers on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"summarizing and paraphrasing information from two sources and providing a list of sources",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Here are two sources about counting large things. Use both.</p>${twoSources()}<ol class="qlist"><li>Summarize Source 1 in one sentence of your own.</li><li>Paraphrase one fact from Source 2. Name the source inside your sentence.</li><li>Write one sentence that uses a fact from each source, and make clear which fact came from where.</li><li>Write a list of your two sources at the bottom of the page.</li></ol>`,key:["A one-sentence summary of Source 1 in the student's own words, such as: Nadia Olsen says to split the area into equal squares, count and record one square at a time, and make the squares smaller if two counters disagree. (W.5.8, summarizing)","A paraphrase of a Source 2 fact with the source named in the sentence, such as: according to Hugo Breen, libraries usually sample shelves instead of counting everything, which lands within about five percent when at least ten shelves are used. The wording has to be the student's own. (W.5.8, paraphrasing)","One sentence drawing on both, with each fact attributed, such as: Olsen's square method is built for things that move, while Breen's sampling method is built for things that stay still, like library shelves. (W.5.8, gathering relevant information from several sources)","A list naming both sources: Olsen, Field Methods for Young Scientists, 2022; and Breen, Statewide Library Service website, 2024. Any clear and consistent form counts as long as both are named. (W.5.8, providing a list of sources)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Present Your Number",short:"Present",parts:[{std:["SL.5.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:100,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a short spoken report on one topic, with ideas in a logical order and facts and details that back a main idea",html:()=>`<p class="big">At the last assembly, each fifth grader stands up and presents one number from the wall.</p>${NUMBERS()}<p class="big">Pick one number. Say what it counts, how somebody would have counted it, why it is worth putting on the wall, and one thing it does not tell you.</p>${pills("My number","How it was counted","Why it belongs on the wall","What it does not tell you")}`,key:["Picks one number and stays with it, rather than running through several (SL.5.4)","Ideas come in a logical order: what the number counts, how it was counted, why it matters, and the limit of it (SL.5.4)","Uses appropriate facts and descriptive details to back a main idea about the number, rather than only reading the table row out (SL.5.4)","Names something the number does not tell you, such as which students read the books, whether the gloves were ever returned, or how the harvest was spread across the year (SL.5.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace, loud enough to hear, and looks at the camera at least some of the time (SL.5.4)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Wall Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Hallway or front window?"},std:["W.5.1"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:170,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Next year the wall of numbers can stay in the hallway, where only the school sees it, or it can move to the front window, where the whole street sees it. The hallway has forty feet of space. The front window has twelve, so about two thirds of the numbers would have to come off.</p><p class="big">Where should the wall go? Write your opinion with reasons and evidence. Write 7 or 8 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open by naming the choice and stating your opinion clearly","Give reasons in an order that makes sense, with evidence for each one","Use words and phrases that link your opinion to your reasons: because, in addition, for that reason, as a result","Say what somebody who disagrees would argue","End with a conclusion that follows from your reasons"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and states an opinion (W.5.1a)","Creates an organizational structure in which ideas are logically grouped to support the writer's purpose (W.5.1a)","Supports the opinion with logically ordered reasons backed by facts and details, such as the forty feet against the twelve, or who actually walks past each spot (W.5.1b)","Links the opinion and the reasons using words, phrases, and clauses such as because, in addition, for that reason, as a result (W.5.1c)","Provides a concluding statement related to the opinion presented (W.5.1d)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"story",label:"Story",icon:"✨",hint:"One job left on the last afternoon"},std:["W.5.3"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:170,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">It is the last afternoon of the school year. Everyone else has finished. You have one job left, and it is going to take longer than the time you have.</p><p class="big">Write the story of that hour. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${pills("At first","Then","After that","By the end")}${checklist(["Set up the situation: who is telling it, where they are, and what the one job is","Tell the events in an order the reader can follow","Use dialogue or a description of what the narrator is thinking","Use words that show time moving: at first, a few minutes later, by the time, finally","Use precise words and details the reader can see and hear","End in a way that follows from what happened"])}`,key:["Orients the reader by establishing a situation and a narrator, and introduces a natural sequence of events (W.5.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, description, and pacing to develop the events or the narrator's response to them (W.5.3b)","Uses a variety of transitional words and phrases to manage the sequence of events (W.5.3c)","Uses concrete words and phrases and sensory details to convey events precisely (W.5.3d)","Provides a conclusion that follows from the narrated experiences or events (W.5.3e)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Wall Arithmetic",short:"Expressions",parts:[{title:"Inside the Brackets",std:["5.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Work on your paper. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"evaluating numerical expressions with parentheses and brackets, and placing grouping symbols",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.5rem"><li>3 &times; (18 + 12)</li><li>2 &times; [ (40 &minus; 15) + 5 ]</li><li>Put parentheses into 8 + 4 &times; 5 so that the value is 60.</li></ol>`,key:["90. Inside the parentheses first: 18 + 12 = 30, then 3 x 30 = 90. Partial: 66 comes from multiplying before adding and does not count.","60. Innermost first: 40 - 15 = 25, then 25 + 5 = 30, then 2 x 30 = 60. Partial: 30 found by stopping inside the brackets is approaching.","(8 + 4) x 5 = 60. Without the parentheses the expression is 8 + 20 = 28, so the grouping is what changes it. Partial: parentheses placed anywhere that does not give 60 does not count."]},{title:"Ten Times and a Tenth",std:["5.NBT.A.1"],n:2,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Say how the two values compare.",desc:"a digit is worth ten times the place to its right and one tenth of the place to its left",html:()=>`<p class="huge" style="text-align:center;letter-spacing:.06em">9,400 &nbsp; 940 &nbsp; 0.55</p>`,key:["Say: In 9,400 the 4 sits in the hundreds place. In 940 the 4 sits in the tens place. How do the two values compare? (400 and 40. The 4 in 9,400 is worth ten times the 4 in 940, and the one in 940 is worth one tenth of it.)","Say: Look at 0.55. How does the value of the first 5 compare with the value of the second 5? (0.5 and 0.05. The first is ten times the second, and the second is one tenth of the first. The rule carries on to the right of the decimal point.)"]},{title:"Decimals to Thousandths",std:["5.NBT.A.3"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Write your answers on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading, writing and comparing decimals to thousandths",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Three questions about decimals. Write each answer.</p>`,qs:["Write three hundred four and seventy-two thousandths in numerals.","Write 5.608 in expanded form.","Compare 0.47 and 0.407. Write it with a greater than or less than sign."],key:["Say: Write three hundred four and seventy-two thousandths in numerals. (304.072. Watch for 304.72, which is seventy-two hundredths.)","Say: Write 5.608 in expanded form. (5 + 0.6 + 0.008, or 5 + 6/10 + 8/1000. Writing the zero hundredths term as well is fine.)","Say: Compare 0.47 and 0.407. Write it with a greater than or less than sign. (0.47 > 0.407. Writing 0.47 as 0.470 makes it plain. A student who says 0.407 is bigger because it has more digits has the common error.)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Fractions on the Wall",short:"Fractions",parts:[{title:"Unlike Bottoms",std:["5.NF.A.1"],n:2,dur:55,dir:"Work on your paper. Show your renaming and hold it up.",desc:"adding and subtracting fractions and mixed numbers with unlike denominators",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.5rem"><li>2/3 + 1/4</li><li>3 1/2 &minus; 1 3/4</li></ol>`,key:["11/12. Rename both with twelfths: 8/12 + 3/12 = 11/12. Or: twenty-fourths work too, 16/24 + 6/24 = 22/24 = 11/12. Partial: adding across does not count.","1 3/4. Rename in fourths: 3 1/2 is 14/4 and 1 3/4 is 7/4, and 14/4 - 7/4 = 7/4, which is 1 3/4. Or: regroup instead, 3 2/4 becomes 2 6/4, then take 1 3/4 away.","Rate on whether the student renames to a common denominator, not only on the final number."]},{title:"A Fraction of a Fraction",std:["5.NF.B.4"],n:4,dur:130,dir:"Work on your paper. Say each answer out loud. Questions 3 and 4 go with the square picture below.",desc:"multiplying a whole number by a fraction and a fraction by a fraction, including the area of a rectangle with fractional sides",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist" style="font-size:1.5rem"><li>What is 2/3 of 15?</li><li>3/4 &times; 2/5</li><li>The square in the picture is one square yard, cut into fourths across and thirds down. The shaded block is 3/4 of a yard by 2/3 of a yard. What is its area in square yards? Use the small rectangles in the picture to show why.</li><li>A raised bed is 2 1/2 feet by 1 1/2 feet. What is its area?</li></ol><div class="figure">${group(areaModel(4,3,3,2,"3/4 yd","2/3 yd"),"The planted corner")}</div>`,key:["10. 15 divided into 3 equal parts is 5, and 2 of those parts is 10. Or 2/3 x 15 = 30/3 = 10. Partial: 5 is one part and not two, so it is approaching. (5.NF.B.4a)","3/10. 3 x 2 = 6 on the top and 4 x 5 = 20 on the bottom, and 6/20 reduces to 3/10. Or: cancel the 2 into the 4 first, which leaves 3/10. (5.NF.B.4a)","1/2 of a square yard. Cutting the square into 4 columns and 3 rows makes 12 small rectangles, and each one is 1/12 of a square yard. The shaded block covers 3 columns by 2 rows, which is 6 of them, and 6/12 = 1/2. In numbers, 3/4 x 2/3 = 6/12 = 1/2. Partial: 6/12 right with no simpler form, or with no use of the picture, is approaching. (5.NF.B.4b, area by tiling with unit fraction pieces)","3 3/4 square feet. 2 1/2 = 5/2 and 1 1/2 = 3/2, and (5 x 3)/(2 x 2) = 15/4 = 3 3/4. Or: split it into 2 x 1 1/2 = 3 and 1/2 x 1 1/2 = 3/4, then add. (5.NF.B.4b, fractional side lengths)","Question 3 is the one to watch: multiplying two fractions that are each less than 1 gives an answer smaller than either of them, which surprises students. Watch too for a unit of plain yards instead of square yards."]}]},{id:18,title:"Several Sources on Counting",short:"Integrate",parts:[{std:["RI.5.9"],n:3,dur:65,dir:"Use the article and both sources together. Answer out loud.",desc:"integrating information from several texts on the same topic in order to speak knowledgeably",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Three texts, one topic: how to count something big. Put them together.</p>${twoSources()}${lookBack("How to Count Something Big",countHtml(true))}`,qs:["Using all three texts, tell me the two different methods for counting something big and when you would use each one.","The article and Source 1 agree on one rule about the numbers. What is it?","Source 2 says sampling lands within about five percent when at least ten shelves are sampled. The article says good counters pick several shelves and compare them. Put those together. What would you tell a librarian who sampled only one shelf?"],key:["Say: Using all three texts, tell me the two different methods for counting something big and when you would use each one. (Splitting an area into squares and counting square by square, for things that move, such as a crowd. Sampling a few shelves and multiplying, for things that stay still, such as books. Both texts and the article have to show up in the answer.)","Say: The article and Source 1 agree on one rule about the numbers. What is it? (Write each number down the moment it is made, before moving on. The article says a number carried across a room is a guess, and Olsen says to record each square before moving to the next.)","Say: Source 2 says sampling lands within about five percent when at least ten shelves are sampled. The article says good counters pick several shelves and compare them. Put those together. What would you tell a librarian who sampled only one shelf? (That one shelf is not enough to trust, because the estimate is only as good as the shelf picked, and the five percent figure depends on sampling at least ten and comparing them. This conclusion is in neither text on its own.)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Explain the Wall",short:"Explain",parts:[{std:["W.5.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short informative piece that develops a topic with facts, definitions and linking words",html:()=>`<p class="big">Explain to a visitor what the wall of numbers is and how a school would put one together. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Open by naming the topic and saying what the piece will cover","Group the facts so one idea leads to the next","Define one term a visitor would not know, such as sampling","Use linking words: for example, in addition, because, as a result","End with a concluding statement"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and previews what follows (W.5.2a)","Develops the topic with facts, concrete details, or examples drawn from the texts or the wall, such as the 9,400 books, the 212 pounds, or counting a crowd square by square (W.5.2b)","Includes a definition or an explanation of at least one term, and groups related information rather than listing it at random (W.5.2a, W.5.2b)","Links ideas with words and phrases such as for example, in addition, because, as a result, and ends with a concluding statement (W.5.2c, W.5.2e)","Five or six sentences is enough. This is a short check of the shape of an explanation, not a full report."]}]},{id:20,title:"The Balloon Table",short:"Tiny Bits",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model of particles too small to be seen to explain what happens to matter",html:()=>`<p class="big">Balloons filled with helium for the end of year showcase float against the ceiling on Monday. By Thursday they are drifting about at head height, with no holes and no knots undone.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("balloons",84)}${F.icon("confetti",84)}${F.icon("trophy",84)}</div>`,qs:["No holes and no undone knots. Explain where the helium went.","A balloon filled with ordinary air in the same room is still the same size on Thursday. What does that suggest about helium particles compared with air particles?","Fruit squash is being poured at the far table. Explain how you can smell it from the other side of the hall."],key:["The helium particles are small enough to pass between the particles of the rubber itself, a few at a time, so gas leaks out through the material without any hole. (5-PS1-1)","That helium particles are smaller than the particles that make up most of the air, so they slip through the rubber far more easily. Both are gases, but their particles are not the same size. (5-PS1-1)","Particles of the smell substances leave the squash and spread out through the air, moving between air particles until enough of them reach your nose. They are far too small to see. (5-PS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying materials from measured properties rather than from appearance",html:()=>`<p class="big">A mystery materials booth at the showcase. Visitors test four identical-looking white blocks and read off the chart.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Block</th><th>Mass of a 10 cubic cm piece</th><th>Floats?</th><th>Conducts heat quickly?</th><th>Dissolves in warm water?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>0.4 g</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>9 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>21 g</td><td>no</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>27 g</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The four blocks are polystyrene foam, hard white plastic, a block of pressed salt, and painted aluminum.</p>`,qs:["Which block is the aluminum, and which result settles it?","Which block is the pressed salt? Name the test that shows it and say why that test is decisive.","Block 1 is only 0.4 g for the same size as block 4's 27 g. What is block 1, and what does that tell you about what is inside it?"],key:["4. It is the only one that carries heat quickly along its length, which is what a metal does. (5-PS1-3)","3. It is the only one that dissolves in warm water, and dissolving is a property no plastic, foam, or metal on this list shares. (5-PS1-3)","The polystyrene foam. It is mostly trapped air with a small amount of plastic, which is why the same volume weighs a fraction of the others and why it floats. (5-PS1-3)"]}]},{id:21,title:"The Sealed Bag Demonstration",short:"Weigh In",parts:[{std:["5-PS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"using measurements to show that total weight is conserved through heating, cooling, and mixing",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase demonstration. Three sealed bags were weighed, something happened inside each, and they were weighed again. No bag was opened.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Sealed bag</th><th>What happened inside</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>baking soda and vinegar mixed; the bag puffed up tight</td><td>172 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>an ice cube left to melt in warm hands</td><td>64 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>sugar shaken into water until nothing could be seen</td><td>231 g</td><td>231 g</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Bag 1 puffed up tight with gas. What does it weigh afterwards? Explain.","What does bag 2 weigh once the ice has melted?","A visitor says bag 1 must be heavier because it is now full of gas. Explain what is wrong with that."],key:["172 g. A gas formed, but it was made from the baking soda and vinegar already inside, and it is still in the sealed bag, so nothing was gained or lost. (5-PS1-2)","64 g. Melting rearranges the particles from solid to liquid without adding or removing any. (5-PS1-2)","The gas was not added from outside. It was made out of the substances already in the bag, so the total matter in there is unchanged. The bag got bigger, not heavier. (5-PS1-2)"]},{std:["5-PS1-4"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"deciding from evidence whether mixing two substances made a new substance",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three mixings on the showcase demonstration table, with what visitors observed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Mixing</th><th>What was seen</th></tr>
     <tr><td>a steel nail in a cup of copper sulfate solution, left a day</td><td>the nail came out coated with a pinkish brown layer and the blue of the liquid faded</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sand, salt, and iron filings shaken together</td><td>a magnet pulled out the filings, water dissolved the salt out, and the sand was left; all three recovered unchanged</td></tr>
     <tr><td>cream shaken hard in a sealed jar for ten minutes</td><td>solid yellow butter in a thin watery liquid, and more shaking never turned it back into cream</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which two produced a new substance? Give the evidence for each.","Which one is a mixture, and name the three separations used to prove it.","A visitor says the butter is a new substance because it looks different. Give the better reason, from the chart."],key:["The nail in copper sulfate, because a new pinkish brown substance appeared on the nail and the blue color of the liquid faded, and the shaken cream, because the change to butter cannot be reversed by more shaking. (5-PS1-4)","The sand, salt, and iron filings. A magnet pulled out the filings, water dissolved the salt out, and the sand was left, and all three came back unchanged. (5-PS1-4)","Looking different is not enough on its own, because stirred sand in water looks different too. The real reason is that the change cannot be undone: no amount of shaking brings the cream back. (5-PS1-4)"]}]},{id:22,title:"The Year-Long Plant Poster",short:"Plant Food",parts:[{std:["5-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the poster data. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing from measurements that plants get their material for growth from air and water",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase poster reports a whole year of measurements on one sunflower grown in a sealed pot of weighed soil.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sprout",76)}${F.icon("sunflower",88)}${F.icon("board",76)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Start</th><th>End</th><th>Change</th></tr>
     <tr><td>plant, dried</td><td>0.2 g</td><td>412 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dry soil</td><td>3,000 g</td><td>2,994 g</td><td>?</td></tr>
     <tr><td>water given in total</td><td></td><td>61 liters</td><td></td></tr></table>`,qs:["Fill in the change column for the plant and for the soil.","The poster's conclusion says the plant was built from air and water. Explain how these numbers support it.","A visitor asks why 61 liters of water were needed if the plant only weighs 412 g. Answer that."],key:["The plant gained about 412 g. The soil lost 6 g. (5-LS1-1)","The plant gained about 412 g while the soil lost only 6 g, which is nowhere near enough to account for the growth. The only other things that entered were air, water, and sunlight, so the material must have come mainly from carbon dioxide in the air and from water. (5-LS1-1)","Most of the water was not built into the plant. It was drawn up through the roots and passed out of the leaves as vapor, which is how a plant moves minerals and keeps cool. Only a small part of the water ends up as plant material. (5-LS1-1)"]},{std:["5-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Look at the poster. Answer out loud.",desc:"using a model to trace the energy in an animal's food back to the sun",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase poster traces the energy in the snacks on the refreshment table.</p>
     <p class="huge" style="font-size:2.2rem;margin:10px 0;text-align:center">${F.icon("sun",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("wheat",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("cow",46)} &rarr; ${F.icon("cheese",46)}</p>
     <p class="big">The cheese cubes came from a cow fed on hay and grain. The bread came straight from grain.</p>`,qs:["Trace the energy in a cheese cube back to its source.","The bread is two steps from the sun and the cheese is three. Explain what that means for how much of the sun's energy reaches you.","A visitor says the cow turns grass into energy. Correct that sentence."],key:["From the cow, which got it from the hay and grain, which the plants made using energy from sunlight. (5-PS3-1)","Each extra step loses most of the energy, because the animal uses it for moving, growing, repairing its body, and keeping warm. So less of the sun's original energy reaches you through the cheese than through the bread. (5-PS3-1)","The cow does not turn grass into energy, because the energy was already in the grass. The cow releases and uses that stored energy, which came from sunlight, and stores a small part of it in milk. (5-PS3-1)"]}]},{id:23,title:"The School Garden Model",short:"Matter Cycle",parts:[{std:["5-LS2-1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Look at the model. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with a model how matter moves among plants, animals, decomposers, and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase model of the school garden over one year, with the arrows that matter actually followed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>From</th><th>To</th><th>How</th></tr>
     <tr><td>air and soil</td><td>garden plants</td><td>carbon dioxide, water, and minerals taken in</td></tr>
     <tr><td>garden plants</td><td>insects, birds, and the school</td><td>eaten</td></tr>
     <tr><td>trimmings, roots, fallen fruit, and waste</td><td>worms, fungi, and bacteria in the compost bay</td><td>broken down</td></tr>
     <tr><td>finished compost</td><td>the beds</td><td>dug in each spring</td></tr>
     <tr><td>beds</td><td>next year's plants</td><td>minerals taken up by roots</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("carrot",70)}${F.icon("bluebird",70)}${F.icon("mushroom",70)}${F.icon("sprout",70)}</div>`,qs:["Name the decomposers in the model and say what makes them decomposers rather than animals that eat plants.","The compost bay shrinks to about a third of its height over the winter. Where did the missing material go?","The school sold all its vegetables at a stall and brought nothing back to the beds. Predict what the model would show after several years.","Explain why this model is better drawn as a circle than as a line."],key:["Worms, fungi, and bacteria. They feed on dead material and waste and break it into simpler substances, rather than eating living plants or animals. (5-LS2-1)","Most of it left as gas, mainly carbon dioxide and water vapor, released by the decomposers as they broke the material down. A little also drained out as liquid. (5-LS2-1)","The minerals carried off in the vegetables would never come back, so the soil would get poorer year after year and the crops would get smaller unless compost or fertilizer were brought in from somewhere else. (5-LS2-1)","Because the last arrow leads back to where the first one started. The same matter goes from air and soil into plants, through animals and decomposers, and back into the soil and air to be used again. A line would suggest it is used once and gone. (5-LS2-1)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Hanging the Banner",short:"Down",parts:[{std:["5-PS2-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the gravitational force Earth exerts on objects is directed down",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase banner is being hung across the hall. A weight on a string is held against the wall to find the vertical, and a dropped program skids under the table.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("flag",80)}${F.icon("books",80)}${F.icon("confetti",80)}</div>`,qs:["Which way does the dropped program go, and which way does the weighted string hang?","The banner sags in the middle even though both ends are pulled tight. Which way does it sag, and why that way?","Describe one experiment you could do in this hall to show that Earth's pull on an object is directed down. Then say what result would prove it."],key:["Both go straight down toward the floor. (5-PS2-1)","It sags straight down in the middle. Earth pulls on every part of the banner, directed down, and the middle is the part furthest from the two supports, so it is pulled lowest. (5-PS2-1)","Any sound design. For example drop several different objects from the same height, from different spots around the hall, and record which way each one travels. If every object, heavy or light, travels straight down toward the floor from wherever it is released, the pull is directed down everywhere in the room. (5-PS2-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the report. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing with an example how the geosphere, biosphere, hydrosphere, and atmosphere interact",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Earth system has four parts. The geosphere is rock and soil. The biosphere is living things. The hydrosphere is all the water. The atmosphere is air.</p>
     <p class="big">A showcase report on the schoolyard, one year on from a planting project. Rain used to run straight off the bare slope into the drain, taking soil with it, and the drain backed up twice. A dug swale and a planted bank now hold the rain. The planted area is measurably cooler on hot afternoons, worms have appeared in soil that used to be bare and hard, and birds use the new hedge.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("rain",72)}${F.icon("tree",72)}${F.icon("bluebird",72)}${F.icon("drop",72)}</div>`,qs:["Name the two spheres interacting when rain washed soil off the bare slope.","Pick one change in the report that shows the biosphere altering another sphere, and describe it.","The planted area is cooler on hot afternoons. Explain which spheres are involved and how."],key:["The hydrosphere and the geosphere. Water running over the land picked up and carried away soil. The atmosphere delivered the rain. (5-ESS2-1)","Any supported answer. Plant roots and the planted bank hold the soil of the geosphere and slow the hydrosphere so rain soaks in, and the plants also changed the soil enough for worms to live in it. (5-ESS2-1)","The biosphere and the atmosphere, with the geosphere included. Leaves block sunlight and release water vapor, which cools the air and the ground beneath them. (5-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:25,title:"The Star Brightness Display",short:"Sky",parts:[{std:["5-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:70,dir:"Read the display. Answer out loud.",desc:"arguing that the sun looks brightest because of how near it is, not because it gives off the most light",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase display on why the sun looks so much brighter than any other star.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Star</th><th>Light it gives off</th><th>Distance from Earth</th><th>How bright it looks from here</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the sun</td><td>1 unit</td><td>8 light-minutes</td><td>overwhelming</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Proxima Centauri, the nearest star after the sun</td><td>about 0.002 units</td><td>4.2 light-years</td><td>too faint to see without a telescope</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Rigel</td><td>about 100,000 units</td><td>about 860 light-years</td><td>a bright point</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sun",76)}${F.icon("star",76)}${F.icon("telescope",76)}</div>`,qs:["Which star on the display gives off the least light? Which gives off the most? Which looks brightest from here?","Proxima Centauri is the nearest star after the sun and still needs a telescope. Explain how both of its numbers contribute to that.","Write the one sentence you would put at the top of this display to state its main point."],key:["Proxima Centauri gives off the least. Rigel gives off the most. The sun looks by far the brightest. (5-ESS1-1)","It gives off very little light, about two thousandths of the sun's, and it is 4.2 light-years away, which is still an enormous distance. A faint star that far off delivers almost nothing to the eye. (5-ESS1-1)","Something like: how bright a star looks from Earth depends on its distance as much as on the light it gives off, which is why the sun outshines far more powerful stars. (5-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["5-ESS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the poster. Answer out loud.",desc:"finding patterns in graphed data about shadows, day length, and the night sky",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase poster of a whole year of measurements on the same playground post.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Month measured</th><th>Noon shadow</th><th>Hours of daylight</th><th>A group of stars overhead at 10 p.m.</th></tr>
     <tr><td>December</td><td>340 cm</td><td>9.2</td><td>a bright winter group</td></tr>
     <tr><td>March</td><td>155 cm</td><td>12.1</td><td>a different spring group</td></tr>
     <tr><td>June</td><td>62 cm</td><td>15.0</td><td>a different summer group</td></tr>
     <tr><td>September</td><td>155 cm</td><td>12.2</td><td>a different autumn group</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the two patterns in the shadow and daylight columns, and say how they relate to each other.","March and September have the same shadow and nearly the same daylight, yet different stars overhead at 10 p.m. Explain both facts.","A visitor asks whether the shadow pattern and the star pattern have the same cause. Answer carefully."],key:["The noon shadow is longest in December and shortest in June, and the daylight hours are the reverse, shortest in December and longest in June. They move opposite each other, because both depend on how high the midday sun climbs. (5-ESS1-2)","The shadow and the daylight match because the midday sun reaches the same height at both equinoxes. The stars differ because Earth is on opposite sides of its orbit in March and September, so at 10 p.m. the night side faces opposite directions in space. (5-ESS1-2)","Not quite the same cause. Both come from Earth's motion, but the shadow and daylight pattern comes from Earth's tilt as it orbits, which changes how high the sun climbs, while the changing stars come from Earth's position moving round its orbit, which changes which way the night side faces. Earth's daily spin is what moves the shadow within one day. (5-ESS1-2)"]}]},{id:26,title:"The Water Display",short:"Water",parts:[{std:["5-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the display. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the amounts and percentages of water and fresh water in Earth's reservoirs",html:()=>`<p class="big">A showcase display uses 100 small jars to stand for all the water on Earth.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Reservoir</th><th>Jars out of 100</th><th>Fresh or salty?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>ocean</td><td>97</td><td>salty</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ice caps and glaciers</td><td>2</td><td>fresh, frozen</td></tr>
     <tr><td>groundwater</td><td>1</td><td>mostly fresh</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lakes, rivers, soil, air, and living things</td><td>a few drops in the hundredth jar</td><td>mostly fresh</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How many of the 100 jars hold fresh water, and how many of those hold fresh water that is not frozen?","Turn the jar model into percentages and state the two headline figures.","A visitor says the display is wrong because the last row has no whole jar. Explain why the display is right and suggest how to make that row visible."],key:["Three jars hold fresh water, and about one of those holds fresh water that is not frozen, plus the few drops of surface water. (5-ESS2-2)","About 97% of Earth's water is salty ocean and about 3% is fresh, of which only about 1% of the total is liquid fresh water. (5-ESS2-2)","The display is right: lakes, rivers, soil, air, and living things really do hold far less than one hundredth of Earth's water, so a few drops is the honest amount. To make it visible, add a second display of only the fresh water, where the surface water becomes a visible slice. (5-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["5-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two project reports. Answer out loud.",desc:"combining information about ways communities use science to protect resources and the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two schools reported their year-long projects at the showcase.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>School</th><th>Project</th><th>Result after one year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Hill Road</td><td>sorted all lunch waste, composted the food scraps on site, and used the compost in the garden</td><td>waste to landfill down by 55%, and the garden produced twice as much</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Riverside</td><td>fitted spring-loaded taps and fixed every dripping cistern, then published the water meter reading each week</td><td>water use down 30%, and pupils began reporting leaks themselves</td></tr>
     </table>`,qs:["Hill Road's garden produced twice as much. Explain how composting lunch waste led to that.","Riverside published the meter reading each week and pupils began reporting leaks. Why would publishing a number change what people do?","Combine the two reports into one plan for a third school. Then say which single step you would do first and why."],key:["The food scraps were broken down by decomposers into compost, which returned minerals to the garden soil, so the plants had more of what they needed and grew better. (5-ESS3-1)","It made the result visible and shared, so people could see whether their effort was working and could tell when something was wrong. Information people can see changes what they notice and what they bother to report. (5-ESS3-1)","Sort and compost the food waste on site and use the compost in the garden, fit spring-loaded taps and repair leaks, and publish the meter reading weekly. Any first step with a reason, for instance fixing leaks first because it saves water immediately and costs nothing to maintain, or composting first because it needs no plumbing work. (5-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Design a Display Stand",short:"Design It",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Listen, then answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",read:"The showcase is held outdoors on the field. Last year the poster boards blew over twice and three posters were ruined by a shower. The organizers want a stand that holds a poster board upright in a stiff breeze and keeps it dry in a passing shower. One pupil must be able to carry it across the field, it has to be built from cardboard tubes, timber offcuts, string, and clear plastic sheet already in the store, and it has to be finished in two afternoons.",qs:["Say the problem in one sentence.","Name three things the finished stand must do to count as a success.","Name two limits the organizers have to work inside."],key:["Poster boards blow over and get wet at the outdoor showcase, so the organizers need a stand that holds a board upright and keeps it dry. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any three criteria from the story: holds a poster board upright in a stiff breeze, keeps the poster dry in a passing shower, and can be carried across the field by one pupil. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two constraints: only cardboard tubes, timber offcuts, string, and clear plastic sheet from the store, and it must be finished in two afternoons. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing possible solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three designs, each tested with the same board, the same fan on its highest setting, and the same watering can shower.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Stayed up in the fan test</th><th>Poster dry after the shower</th><th>Mass to carry</th><th>Build time</th><th>Store materials only?</th></tr>
     <tr><td>P: a flat A-frame of timber offcuts</td><td>yes</td><td>no, the top edge soaked</td><td>4 kg</td><td>1 afternoon</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q: A-frame with a sloping clear plastic hood over the top</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>5 kg</td><td>2 afternoons</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>R: a bought weighted display frame with a cover</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>17 kg</td><td>none, delivered</td><td>no</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which designs keep the poster dry? Which of those also fits inside both limits?","Design P builds in one afternoon and is lightest. Name the criterion it fails and say why that matters here.","Design R needs no building at all. Name the two reasons it is ruled out."],key:["Q and R both keep the poster dry. Only Q also fits the limits, since it uses store materials and builds in the two afternoons available. (3-5-ETS1-2)","It does not keep the poster dry; the top edge soaked. Three posters were ruined by rain last year, so staying dry is one of the things the stand exists to do. (3-5-ETS1-2)","It has to be bought rather than built from store materials, and at 17 kg one pupil cannot carry it across the field. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the test plan. Answer out loud.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming the failure points to improve",html:()=>`<p class="big">The builders tested how wide to set the feet of the A-frame.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Trial 1</th><th>Trial 2</th></tr>
     <tr><td>distance between the feet</td><td>40 cm</td><td>80 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>board size and mass</td><td>same board both times</td><td>same board both times</td></tr>
     <tr><td>fan setting and distance</td><td>highest, 2 m away</td><td>highest, 2 m away</td></tr>
     <tr><td>seconds before it tipped</td><td>tipped after 4 s</td><td>did not tip in 60 s</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what went wrong</td><td>tipped backwards away from the fan</td><td>the wide feet stuck out into the walkway and were tripped over</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What was held the same, and what was changed? Why does that make the test fair?","Suppose trial 2 had also moved the fan to 3 m away. Could you still say the wider feet were the reason it stayed up? Explain.","Name a change that would fix the failure in each trial, and say which trial you would develop further."],key:["The board, the fan setting, and the fan distance were all held the same, and only the distance between the feet changed, so the difference in tipping can be traced to the foot width. (3-5-ETS1-3)","No. Two things would have changed, the foot width and the fan distance, so the stand might have stayed up only because the wind was weaker. That is not a fair test. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Trial 1 tipped, so widen the feet, add a weight at the base, or angle the board into the wind. Trial 2 obstructed the walkway, so turn the stand so the feet run along the walkway rather than across it, use a weighted narrow base instead, or mark the feet clearly. Trial 2 is the one to develop, since it solved the main problem and its failure is easier to fix. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Then and Now",short:"Then and Now",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.1"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the scale of pre-Columbian settlement and the nations that continue today",html:()=>`<p class="big">A review of what you know about North America before 1492, and what became of it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
     <tr><td>how many people</td><td>historians estimate millions of people living across North America, in hundreds of separate nations</td></tr>
     <tr><td>how many languages</td><td>hundreds, belonging to many unrelated language families</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the largest settlements</td><td>towns of thousands of people, such as the mound city at Cahokia on the Mississippi</td></tr>
     <tr><td>what happened after 1492</td><td>illnesses brought from Europe, war, and loss of land reduced the population enormously over the following centuries</td></tr>
     <tr><td>today</td><td>hundreds of American Indian nations continue, with their own governments, citizens, and lands, and many are working to keep or revive their languages</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two different pieces of evidence that North America before 1492 held many separate nations rather than one people.","Cahokia was a town of thousands. What does a settlement that size require of the people who built it?","The fourth row and the fifth row are both true. Explain how to hold both at once.","Why does it matter that the last row is written in the present tense?"],key:["Any two: hundreds of different languages from unrelated families, hundreds of separate governments and territories, and completely different ways of building, farming, and living from region to region. Partial: one piece of evidence named is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Organized farming to feed thousands, planning and shared labor to build the mounds, a system for making decisions and settling disputes, and trade to bring in what the area did not produce. Partial: one requirement named is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","An enormous loss of life and land happened, and the nations were not wiped out. Both the scale of the catastrophe and the fact that these nations continue today have to be said, because leaving out either one tells it wrongly. Partial: one of the two rows accounted for is approaching. (HSS-5.1)","Because these are living nations with members, governments, and lands now. Speaking of them only in the past suggests they are gone, which is not true and is unfair to people who are still here. Partial: saying they are still here with nothing said about why the tense matters is approaching. (HSS-5.1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"What the Voyages Set in Motion",short:"Voyages",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.2"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the consequences of the early explorations of the Americas for peoples on both sides",html:()=>`<p class="big">A review of the voyages and what followed from them.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Consequence</th><th>For whom</th></tr>
     <tr><td>new foods such as potatoes and maize spread worldwide and populations grew</td><td>Europe, Africa, and Asia</td></tr>
     <tr><td>silver and gold shipped across the Atlantic funded European states and wars</td><td>European powers</td></tr>
     <tr><td>illnesses never known there before killed an enormous share of the population</td><td>the peoples of the Americas</td></tr>
     <tr><td>land claimed, settled, and taken over four centuries</td><td>American Indian nations</td></tr>
     <tr><td>millions of people taken from Africa and enslaved in the Americas</td><td>West and Central Africa</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name one consequence that was an improvement for the people it affected, and one that was a catastrophe.","Potatoes from the Americas helped European populations grow. Explain how a single new crop could do that.","The voyages are often described as discovery. Explain why many people object to that word.","Pick one row and explain how its effects were still being felt a hundred years later."],key:["Any supported pair. New foods spreading worldwide helped populations grow, while the illnesses brought to the Americas and the enslavement of millions of Africans were catastrophes. Partial: one of the improvement and the catastrophe named is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Potatoes grow well in poor, cool soil and give far more food per field than the grain they replaced, so the same land could feed more people and fewer went hungry in a bad year. Partial: saying potatoes were popular with no account of food per field is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Because millions of people were already living here and knew the land perfectly well. Discovery describes it only from a European point of view, as though a place is not known until a European sees it. Partial: saying the word is unfair with no account of who was already there is approaching. (HSS-5.2)","Any supported answer. Land taken from nations was still being taken a century and more later; silver shipped to Europe shaped its economies and wars for generations; enslavement built economies and left consequences that outlasted slavery itself. Partial: a row named with no effect carried a century forward is approaching. (HSS-5.2)"]},{std:["HSS-5.3"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the long-term results of cooperation and conflict between American Indian nations and settlers",html:()=>`<p class="big">A review of the relationship over three centuries, and where it stands now.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>What the relationship looked like</th></tr>
     <tr><td>first contact</td><td>trade, shared knowledge, and alliances, alongside illness and early land disputes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the colonial centuries</td><td>treaties made and broken, alliances in European wars, growing settlement pressure, and periods of open conflict</td></tr>
     <tr><td>after 1783</td><td>treaties signed with the United States, removal from homelands, and land set aside and then reduced again</td></tr>
     <tr><td>today</td><td>treaties are still law; nations have their own governments, courts, schools, and citizenship, and they negotiate with the United States government directly</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name one thing that stayed the same across all three of the first periods.","Treaties made in the 1700s and 1800s are still law today. Explain what that means in practice.","The chart says nations negotiate with the United States government directly. Why would that be, rather than through a state?","Looking at the whole chart, explain why both cooperation and conflict are needed to describe this history honestly."],key:["Any supported answer: agreements were made and then broken as settlement pressure grew, and nations kept making their own decisions and acting in their own interests throughout. Partial: naming a feature of one period only is approaching, since the question asks what held across all three. (HSS-5.3)","A treaty is a binding agreement, so rights it set out, over land, water, fishing, hunting, or payments, can still be argued and enforced in court today, and courts have upheld them. History is not only in the past when it is written into law. Partial: saying treaties still matter with no account of rights being enforced in court is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Because the treaties were made with the United States itself, and American Indian nations are recognized as nations with their own governments. That relationship is between two governments, not between a nation and a state. Partial: saying the federal government is bigger is not the reason; naming the treaties with nothing about nation to nation standing is approaching. (HSS-5.3)","Because both really happened, often at the same time and in the same place. Telling only the cooperation hides the loss of land and life, and telling only the conflict hides the trade, alliances, knowledge, and agreements, and makes it look like something that happened to people rather than between them. Partial: one of the two one-sided tellings criticized is approaching. (HSS-5.3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"From Colonies to a Country",short:"Colonies",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.4"],n:4,dur:135,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining what became of the colonial institutions after independence",html:()=>`<p class="big">Many of the institutions the colonies built were carried straight into the new country.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Colonial institution</th><th>What it became</th></tr>
     <tr><td>elected colonial assemblies</td><td>state legislatures, and the model for the House of Representatives</td></tr>
     <tr><td>New England town meetings</td><td>local government that still runs by town meeting in parts of New England</td></tr>
     <tr><td>trial by a jury of local people</td><td>written into the Constitution and the Bill of Rights</td></tr>
     <tr><td>colonial newspapers and printers</td><td>a free press, protected by the First Amendment</td></tr>
     <tr><td>established churches supported by a parish tax</td><td>deliberately ended. Virginia stopped its church tax in 1786, the other states ended theirs over the following decades, and the First Amendment barred Congress from establishing a religion at all</td></tr>
     <tr><td>slavery</td><td>continued, and expanded, until the 13th Amendment in 1865</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two colonial institutions that became part of the new country's government, and say what each became.","Trial by a local jury was a colonial grievance and then a constitutional right. Explain how those two things connect.","Two rows did not simply carry on. One institution was deliberately ended and one continued even though it contradicted the new country's stated principles. Name each and say what happened to it.","Why would the writers of the Constitution build on institutions that already existed rather than invent new ones?"],key:["Any two: colonial assemblies became state legislatures and the model for the House of Representatives; town meetings became local government still used in New England; local juries and a free press were written into the Constitution and the Bill of Rights. Partial: one institution matched to what it became is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Colonists objected when Britain moved trials out of the colony where an event happened, so when they wrote their own rules they put the right to a local jury in writing, to make sure it could not be taken away again. Partial: naming the grievance with no link to the written right is approaching. (HSS-5.4)","Ended on purpose: the established churches supported by a parish tax. Virginia stopped its church tax in 1786, the other states ended theirs over the following decades, and the First Amendment barred Congress from establishing a religion. Continued: slavery, which the new country kept and expanded even though it contradicted the principles the same documents stated, and which ended only with the 13th Amendment in 1865. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so the established churches with nothing on slavery sits there. (HSS-5.4)","Because they already worked, people understood them, and a government built out of familiar institutions was far easier to get agreed and accepted than one invented from nothing. Partial: saying it was easier with nothing said about agreement or acceptance is approaching. (HSS-5.4)"]},{std:["HSS-5.5"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the list. Answer out loud.",desc:"weighing the causes of the American Revolution against one another",html:()=>`<p class="big">Five causes of the American Revolution, in no particular order.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cause</th></tr>
     <tr><td>taxes passed by a Parliament the colonies had not elected</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a century and a half of running their own assemblies</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Parliament closing the port of Boston and stripping the Massachusetts assembly of powers</td></tr>
     <tr><td>ideas about natural rights and the consent of the governed, spread in cheap pamphlets</td></tr>
     <tr><td>British rules that limited colonial trade and western settlement</td></tr>
   </table>`,qs:["Pick the cause you think mattered most and give two pieces of evidence or reasoning for it.","Pick a different cause and make the strongest case you can for that one instead.","Which cause do you think mattered least, and why?","Could the Revolution have been avoided? Name one thing that would have had to be different, and say why."],key:["Any cause, argued. For example self-government for 150 years, because it meant colonists already had the thing they were defending, and because their first objection to every tax was that their own assemblies should have decided it. The quality of the reasoning is what is being judged. Partial: a cause named with one piece of reasoning is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","A real second case, not a repeat. For example the closing of the port of Boston, because it was the event that made the other colonies act together rather than separately. Partial: repeating the first case under a different name is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Any cause with a reason. For example taxes alone, because the amounts were small and some were repealed and the dispute carried on anyway. Partial: a cause named with no reason is approaching. (HSS-5.5)","Any thoughtful answer: colonial representation in Parliament, leaving colonial taxation to the assemblies, not punishing a whole colony for a protest, or not closing off western settlement. The reason matters more than the choice. A student may also argue it was unavoidable given the distance and the habit of self-government. Partial: a yes or no with nothing named that would have had to be different is approaching. (HSS-5.5)"]},{std:["HSS-5.6"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"describing the long-term consequences of the American Revolution and the promises it left unkept",html:()=>`<p class="big">The war ended in 1783. Some of what it set in motion took far longer to work out.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>What the Revolution established</th><th>What was left unfinished</th></tr>
     <tr><td>an independent country with no king</td><td>a weak first government that had to be replaced within eight years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the claim that all men are created equal</td><td>slavery continued and expanded, and ended only in 1865</td></tr>
     <tr><td>government by consent of the governed</td><td>most adults could not vote, and the vote widened only over the next two centuries</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a territory reaching to the Mississippi River</td><td>that land was the territory of nations who had not agreed to any of it</td></tr>
     <tr><td>an example that influenced revolutions elsewhere</td><td>including in Haiti, where enslaved people won their freedom and the United States refused to recognize them for decades</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name one thing the Revolution established and the matching thing it left unfinished.","The words all men are created equal were written in a country where slavery was legal. Explain how people later used those same words.","The last row mentions Haiti. Explain why the United States might have been uncomfortable recognizing it.","Was the Revolution finished in 1783? Give a reason for your answer."],key:["Any matched pair from the chart, for example independence from a king, matched with a first government too weak to work, or government by consent, matched with most adults having no vote. Partial: one of the established and the unfinished named is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","People who were excluded quoted them back. Abolitionists, formerly enslaved writers and speakers, campaigners for women's rights, and later civil rights campaigners all argued that the country had to live up to words it had already written down. Partial: naming the contradiction with nothing said about how the words were later used is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Haiti's revolution was made by enslaved people who freed themselves, and recognizing it would have meant recognizing that a successful rising by enslaved people was legitimate, at a time when slavery was legal and expanding in the United States. Partial: saying it was awkward with no account of who made the revolution is approaching. (HSS-5.6)","Any supported answer, though the chart points one way. The fighting ended and independence was won in 1783, while the promises in the Declaration were not met for most people, so a good answer says the war ended and the argument about what the country would be did not. Partial: a yes or no with no reason is approaching. (HSS-5.6)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Two Plans and a Growing Country",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-5.7"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing the Articles of Confederation with the Constitution and explaining why the Constitution endured",html:()=>`<p class="big">The country had two plans of government in its first fifteen years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>Articles of Confederation, 1781</th><th>Constitution, 1789</th></tr>
     <tr><td>can it collect taxes?</td><td>no, it could only ask the states</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>who makes the laws?</td><td>one house, one vote per state</td><td>two houses, one by population and one by state</td></tr>
     <tr><td>is there a president?</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>are there national courts?</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>how is it changed?</td><td>all thirteen states had to agree, so almost never</td><td>two thirds of Congress and three quarters of the states</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name two powers the Constitution gave the national government that the Articles did not.","Under the Articles all thirteen states had to agree to any change. Explain why that was a serious flaw.","The Constitution has lasted more than two centuries. Using the chart, name two features that help explain that.","Which single change from the Articles to the Constitution do you think mattered most? Give a reason."],key:["Any two: the power to collect taxes, a president to carry out the laws, and national courts. Partial: one power named is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","One state out of thirteen could block any repair, so problems everyone agreed about could not be fixed. A plan that cannot be changed cannot adapt, and it had to be replaced instead. Partial: saying it was too strict with no account of one state blocking a repair is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Any two: an amendment process that is demanding but possible, so the document can change without being replaced; three branches that check each other; and two houses that gave both large and small states a reason to stay in. Partial: one feature named is approaching. (HSS-5.7)","Any supported answer. The taxing power, because without money a government cannot pay debts, defend the country, or do anything it decides. Or the amendment process, because it let the document survive changes nobody in 1787 could have foreseen. (HSS-5.7)"]},{std:["HSS-5.8"],n:4,dur:100,dir:"Read the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing population and settlement patterns in 1790 and 1850",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two censuses, sixty years apart.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>1790</th><th>1850</th></tr>
     <tr><td>total population</td><td>about 3.9 million</td><td>about 23 million</td></tr>
     <tr><td>number of states</td><td>13</td><td>31</td></tr>
     <tr><td>where almost everyone lived</td><td>within a hundred miles or so of the Atlantic coast</td><td>spread west to the Mississippi, with settlement in Texas, Oregon, and California</td></tr>
     <tr><td>share living in towns and cities</td><td>about 1 in 20</td><td>about 1 in 7</td></tr>
     <tr><td>people arriving from abroad each year</td><td>a few thousand</td><td>hundreds of thousands</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the two biggest changes between the two censuses.","The share living in towns went from about 1 in 20 to about 1 in 7. Name two things that caused that.","Eighteen new states joined in sixty years. Explain the process a territory went through to become one.","Name one group whose situation got worse over these sixty years, and explain why."],key:["The population grew roughly six times over, and settlement spread from a coastal strip across to the Mississippi and on to the Pacific, with the number of states rising from 13 to 31. Partial: one of the two changes described is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Any two: mills and factories offering paid work, canals and railroads letting cities be fed and supplied, and large numbers of immigrants arriving in port cities and staying there. Partial: one cause named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","Land was surveyed and sold, Congress appointed a governor, settlers elected their own assembly once the population reached a set level, and at 60,000 people the territory could apply to join as a state equal to the others. Partial: two or three of the four steps named is approaching. (HSS-5.8)","American Indian nations, whose land was surveyed, sold, and settled, who were removed from their homelands, and whose treaties were broken as the states spread west. Accept enslaved people, since slavery expanded westward over the same period and about a million people were forcibly moved. Partial: a group named with no account of why their situation got worse is approaching. (HSS-5.8)"]},{std:["HSS-5.9"],n:5,dur:90,dir:"Answer out loud. A map is fine to look at.",desc:"locating states across the whole country and naming their capitals",html:()=>`<div class="figure">${F.icon("globe",96)}${F.icon("flag",96)}${F.icon("map",96)}</div>
     <p class="big">A review of the fifty states and their capitals.</p>`,qs:["How many states are there, and how many of them are not joined to the rest by land?","Name the capital of your own state, and the capital of one state on each coast.","Name the four states that meet at Four Corners, and name the five that touch the Pacific Ocean.","Name three state capitals that are not the largest city in their state.","Which state has the smallest area, and which has the largest? Name the capital of each."],key:["50 states. Alaska and Hawaii are not joined to the rest by land. Partial: the count right with one of the two states named is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","The student's own state and capital, and any correct east coast and west coast pair, such as Boston, Massachusetts and Sacramento, California. (HSS-5.9)","Four Corners: Utah, Colorado, Arizona, New Mexico. Pacific: California, Oregon, Washington, Alaska, Hawaii. Partial: one of the two lists complete is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Any three correct pairs, such as New York with Albany, California with Sacramento, Illinois with Springfield, Washington with Olympia, Nevada with Carson City, Maryland with Annapolis, or Florida with Tallahassee. Partial: one or two correct pairs is approaching. (HSS-5.9)","Rhode Island is the smallest, capital Providence. Alaska is the largest, capital Juneau. Partial: both states named with one capital missing is approaching. (HSS-5.9)"]}]}];const WC=FLU.split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;const readPart=STOPS[0].parts[0];readPart.n=WC;readPart.key[0]=readPart.key[0].replace("__WC__",WC);readPart.desc=`a ${WC}-word grade-level text read aloud`;const LEVELUPS={2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Divide by a Fraction",std:["6.NS.A.1"],needs:["5.NF.B.3"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say what the answer means in the situation.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>How many 1/4 pound bags can be filled from 3 pounds of beans?</li><li>Work out 2/3 ÷ 1/6 and say what the answer counts.</li><li>A board 7/8 of a yard long is cut into pieces 1/8 of a yard long. How many pieces?</li></ol>`,key:["12 bags. 3 ÷ 1/4 asks how many quarters are in 3, and there are 4 in each pound, so 3 × 4 = 12. (6.NS.A.1, dividing by a fraction)","4. 2/3 ÷ 1/6 = 2/3 × 6/1 = 12/3 = 4, and it counts how many sixths fit inside two thirds. (6.NS.A.1)","7 pieces. 7/8 ÷ 1/8 = 7, because there are 7 eighths in 7/8. (6.NS.A.1, a word problem)"]},4:{id:"L4",title:"Level Up: Cite the Evidence",std:["RL.6.1"],needs:["RL.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Cite the exact words that support each answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>What does Delphine believe a number on the wall is for? Cite two pieces of evidence, and explain what each one shows.</li><li>The story does not say what Ms. Adeyemi thought of Delphine's reason. What can you conclude, and what is your strongest evidence?</li></ol>${F.passage(GLOVE,GLOVE_TITLE,true)}`,key:["She believes a number is there to show the year as it actually was, not to flatter it. Evidence: “a single glove was still something somebody lost,” and “leaving it off the wall made the year look tidier than it really was.” The first shows she counts the singles as real losses, and the second shows she sees leaving them out as making the year look better than it was. (RL.6.1, citing evidence for an explicit idea)","She agreed with it, or at least accepted it. Strongest evidence: “Then she found a marker and wrote Delphine's name in the corner, small, the way the rule said.” The wall's rule is that a number goes up with its counter's name on it, so writing the name is the act of accepting the number, and the pause before it shows she thought about it first. (RL.6.1, citing the strongest evidence for an inference)"]},5:{id:"L5",title:"Level Up: Draw It on the Grid",std:["6.G.A.3"],needs:["5.G.A.1"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Plot the points, then answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Plot (2, 2), (9, 2), (9, 6), and (2, 6) and join them in order. What shape is it?</li><li>Find the length of each side, using the coordinates rather than a ruler.</li><li>What is the area of the shape, in square feet?</li></ol>${F.row([F.coordGrid({max:10,size:380,xLabel:"x",yLabel:"y"}),"A blank hallway grid, in feet"])}`,key:["A rectangle. The four points form two horizontal sides and two vertical sides. (6.G.A.3, drawing a polygon in the coordinate plane)","The horizontal sides are 7 feet, from 9 − 2 = 7. The vertical sides are 4 feet, from 6 − 2 = 4. Subtracting the coordinates that change gives the length of a side that runs along a grid line. (6.G.A.3, side length from coordinates)","28 square feet. 7 × 4 = 28. (6.G.A.3)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Which Quote Is Strongest",std:["RI.6.1"],needs:["RI.5.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words you are choosing.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Claim: a number written down straight away is worth more than a better number remembered. Which single sentence in the article is the strongest evidence for that claim? Why is it stronger than the others?</li><li>The article says the square method is more accurate. Cite the evidence it gives, and explain whether that evidence actually supports the claim.</li></ol>${countHtml(true)}`,key:["“A number carried in your head across a room is not a count. It is a guess that sounds like one.” It is strongest because it states directly that an unrecorded number stops being a count at all, which is exactly what the claim says. Sentences about squares or sampling describe methods but do not say why writing it down matters. (RI.6.1, choosing the strongest evidence)","Evidence: “a mistake inside one square changes one number instead of all of them.” It does support the claim, because accuracy here means how much of the total a single error can spoil, and dividing the job limits the damage of any one mistake. (RI.6.1, citing evidence and judging whether it supports the claim)"]},7:{id:"L7",title:"Level Up: Find the Missing Part",std:["6.EE.B.7"],needs:["5.NF.A.2"],n:3,dir:"Write each equation on your paper, solve it, and check your answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Solve: x + 2/5 = 7/10</li><li>On Monday a crew covered some of the strip. On Tuesday it covered 1/3 more, and by then 5/6 of the strip was done. Write an equation and find how much Monday covered.</li><li>Solve: y − 3/8 = 1/4</li></ol>`,key:["x = 3/10. In tenths, 2/5 is 4/10, so x = 7/10 − 4/10 = 3/10. Check: 3/10 + 4/10 = 7/10. (6.EE.B.7, solving x + p = q with rational numbers)","1/2 of the strip. The equation is m + 1/3 = 5/6. In sixths, 1/3 is 2/6, so m = 5/6 − 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2. Check: 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6. (6.EE.B.7, writing and solving an equation for a word problem)","y = 5/8. In eighths, 1/4 is 2/8, so y = 2/8 + 3/8 = 5/8. Check: 5/8 − 3/8 = 2/8 = 1/4. (6.EE.B.7)"]},10:{id:"L10",title:"Level Up: Pronoun Work",std:["L.6.1"],needs:["L.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Which is right? A: Ms. Adeyemi gave the marker to Ravi and I. B: Ms. Adeyemi gave the marker to Ravi and me. How do you know?</li><li>Fix the vague pronoun: Delphine told Noelle that she had counted wrong.</li><li>Name the intensive pronoun and say what it does: Ms. Adeyemi took the number down herself.</li></ol>`,key:["B. The pronoun is the object of the preposition to, so it takes the objective case: me, not I. Testing it alone makes it clear, since nobody would say “gave the marker to I.” (L.6.1a and L.6.1b, pronoun case)","Any version that makes it clear who counted wrong, such as: Delphine told Noelle that Noelle had counted wrong, or Delphine admitted to Noelle that she herself had counted wrong. As written, she could be either person. (L.6.1d, correcting vague pronouns)","herself. An intensive pronoun adds emphasis to the noun it goes with, here that Ms. Adeyemi and nobody else took it down. The sentence still works without it, which is what tells you it is intensive and not reflexive. (L.6.1b, intensive pronouns)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Fractions of a Cube",std:["6.G.A.2"],needs:["5.MD.C.5"],n:3,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say the unit in every answer.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A box is 2 1/2 feet by 3 feet by 1/2 foot. What is its volume?</li><li>How many cubes with edges 1/2 foot long fit inside that box? Show how that number matches your volume.</li><li>A box is 4 1/2 inches by 2 inches by 1 1/2 inches. What is its volume?</li></ol>`,key:["3 3/4 cubic feet. V = 2 1/2 × 3 × 1/2 = 5/2 × 3 × 1/2 = 15/4 = 3 3/4. (6.G.A.2, volume with fractional edge lengths)","30 cubes. The box is 5 half-feet by 6 half-feet by 1 half-foot, and 5 × 6 × 1 = 30. Each cube has a volume of 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8 of a cubic foot, and 30 × 1/8 = 30/8 = 3 3/4 cubic feet, which matches. (6.G.A.2, packing with unit fraction cubes)","13 1/2 cubic inches. V = 9/2 × 2 × 3/2 = 27/2 = 13 1/2. (6.G.A.2)"]},15:{id:"L15",title:"Level Up: Answer the Other Side",std:["W.6.1"],needs:["W.5.1"],n:3,dir:"Add to the writing on your paper, or say the new sentences out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Add three or four sentences to the opinion you just wrote.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>State the strongest version of the other side's claim, using a real number from the choice you were given.</li><li>Answer it with evidence, not with how you feel about it.</li><li>Rewrite your last sentence so it follows from your argument and keeps a formal tone.</li></ol>`,key:["States the opposing claim at its strongest rather than a weakened version, and uses a real figure such as the twelve feet of window or the forty feet of hallway. (W.6.1a, acknowledging alternate claims)","Answers the opposing claim with relevant evidence and clear reasoning rather than with opinion alone. (W.6.1b, supporting claims with reasoning and relevant evidence)","The closing follows from the argument actually made and keeps a formal style, with no slang and no direct address to the reader. (W.6.1d and W.6.1e, formal style and conclusion)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.5.1","RL.5.4","RI.5.1","W.5.8","RL.6.1","RI.6.1","SL.5.4","RI.5.9"];const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];const STARTERS={"W.5.1":["The wall should ____ because ____.","One reason is ____. The evidence is ____.","In addition, ____.","Somebody who disagrees would say ____.","For that reason, ____."],"W.5.3":["It was ____ when ____.","At first, ____.","A few minutes later, ____.","“____,” I said.","By the time ____, ____."],"W.6.1":["The other side would say ____.","That is true, but ____.","The evidence shows ____."],"W.5.8":["Source 1 says, in my own words, ____.","According to ____, ____.","Source 1 says ____, while Source 2 says ____.","My sources: 1. ____ 2. ____"],"SL.5.2":["In short, the report said ____.","The garden part said ____.","The graph shows ____."],"SL.5.4":["My number is ____, and it counts ____.","Somebody counted it by ____.","It belongs on the wall because ____.","What it does not tell you is ____."],"RL.5.1":["The story says, “____.”","The words “____” show that ____."],"RI.5.1":["The article says, “____.”","I know ____ because the article says, “____.”"],"RL.5.4":["____ means ____.","This is a simile or a metaphor because ____.","I know because the story says ____."],"W.5.2":["The wall of numbers is ____.","First, ____.","____ means ____.","In addition, ____.","As a result, ____."],"RI.5.9":["The article says ____.","Source 1 says ____, and Source 2 says ____.","Putting them together, ____."]};function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function fracHtml(){return F.table(["What to do","How"],[["Add or subtract unlike fractions","rename both with the same bottom number, then add or subtract the top numbers"],["Find a common bottom number","multiply the two bottom numbers, or use the smaller number both go into"],["A fraction as division","a/b means a ÷ b"],["An improper fraction to a mixed number","divide, then write the remainder over the same bottom number"],["Check with a benchmark","is each fraction near 0, near 1/2, or near 1?"]])+`<p class="small">Two fractions can only be added or compared when they are parts of the same size whole.</p>`}function volHtml(){return F.table(["What to find","How"],[["Volume of a box","length × width × height, in cubic units"],["Volume from the base","area of the base × the height"],["A missing edge","volume ÷ the area of the face you know"],["Two boxes joined","find each volume, then add them"],["What a volume counts","how many unit cubes fit inside with no gaps and no overlaps"]])+`<p class="small">Volume is always in cubic units. Area is in square units.</p>`}function gridHtml(){return F.table(["Word","What it means"],[["x-axis","the number line that runs across"],["y-axis","the number line that runs up and down"],["Origin","the point (0, 0), where the two axes cross"],["Ordered pair","(x, y): first the distance along the x-axis, then the distance along the y-axis"],["A point on the y-axis","has an x-coordinate of 0"],["A point on the x-axis","has a y-coordinate of 0"]])+`<p class="small">Travel across first, then up. The order of the two numbers is what tells them apart.</p>`}function rootHtml(){return F.table(["Word part","What it means"],[["circum-","around: circumference"],["trans-","across: transparent"],["inter-","between: interrupted"],["pre-","before: preliminary"],["dis-","apart or away: distribution"],["un-","not: unreliable"],["re-","again or back: responsible"],["-ible, -able","can be: responsible"],["-ant, -ent","one that, or doing: significant, transparent"],["-ion, -tion","the act or result of: distribution"],["-ate","to do: estimate"],["-ory, -ary","a place or thing for: inventory, preliminary"]])+`<p class="small">Cover the beginning and the ending, read the middle, then put the whole word back together and say it smoothly.</p>`}function sentHtml(st){const p=st&&st.parts&&st.parts[0],k=p&&p.alt&&p.alt.key||p&&p.std&&p.std[0]||"W.5.1";const key=k==="opinion"?"W.5.1":k==="story"?"W.5.3":k;return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[key]||STARTERS["W.5.1"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.NS.A.1","6.G.A.2","6.G.A.3","6.EE.B.7","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.4","5.OA.A.1"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const rr=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===rr&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[rr,k];r()})},frac:{name:"Fraction card",std:["5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","6.NS.A.1","6.EE.B.7","5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.4"],html:fracHtml},vol:{name:"Volume card",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],html:volHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid card",std:["5.G.A.1","6.G.A.3"],html:gridHtml},roots:{name:"Root and affix chart",std:["RF.5.3"],html:rootHtml},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:["W.5.1","W.5.3","W.5.8","W.6.1","SL.5.2","SL.5.4","RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.5.4","W.5.2","RI.5.9"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"mult",std:["5.NF.A.2","5.NF.B.3","6.NS.A.1","6.EE.B.7"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products used to rename fractions. Rate on whether the student knows what to rename and why."},{t:"mult",std:["5.MD.C.3","5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2","6.G.A.3"],msg:"The multiplication chart gives the products. Rate on the setup and on whether the answer carries cubic or square units."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.A.2"],msg:"The fraction card gives the method for unlike denominators and names the benchmark check, so count those steps as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.B.3"],msg:"The card states that a fraction is a division and how to turn an improper fraction into a mixed number, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recall."},{t:"frac",std:["6.NS.A.1","6.EE.B.7"],msg:"The card names the method. Rate on whether the student carries it out and can say what the answer means."},{t:"vol",std:["5.MD.C.3"],msg:"The volume card states what a volume counts, so count the definition answers as supported and rate the reasoning about the solids."},{t:"vol",std:["5.MD.C.5","6.G.A.2"],msg:"The card gives both formulas, the missing-edge method, and the rule for two joined boxes. Count those items as supported, not independent."},{t:"grid",std:["5.G.A.1","6.G.A.3"],msg:"The grid card names the axes, the origin, and the order of an ordered pair, so this stop then shows applying stated definitions rather than recalling them."},{t:"roots",std:["RF.5.3"],msg:"The root and affix chart gives the meaning of every prefix, root, and ending on the cards, so count the word-part part of this stop as supported and rate the reading of the whole word."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.1","W.5.3","W.6.1"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.8"],msg:"The frames show how to open a summary, name a source, and list sources, so count the source list as supported."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.2"],msg:"The frames cue the student to start with their own words, so count the summarizing cue as supported and rate whether the content is right and genuinely reworded."},{t:"sent",std:["SL.5.4"],msg:"The frames supply the order of the presentation, so count the organization check as supported and rate the facts and details the student adds."},{t:"sent",std:["RL.5.1","RI.5.1","RL.5.4"],msg:"The frames remind the student to quote. Rate on whether the quotation is accurate and whether it actually supports the answer."},{t:"passage",std:PASSAGE_STD,msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"mult",std:["5.NF.A.1","5.NF.B.4"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products used to rename and to multiply fractions. Rate on whether the student knows what to rename or multiply and why."},{t:"mult",std:["5.OA.A.1"],msg:"The chart gives the products. Rate on the order the student works the grouping symbols in, not on the arithmetic."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.A.1"],msg:"The fraction card gives the method for unlike denominators and how to handle an improper result, so count those steps as supported, not independent."},{t:"frac",std:["5.NF.B.4"],msg:"The card does not give the multiplying rule, but it does give the benchmark check. Note whether the student used it to decide the answer was reasonable."},{t:"sent",std:["W.5.2"],msg:"Sentence frames supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"sent",std:["RI.5.9"],msg:"The frames hand the student the shape of an answer that uses every text. Rate on which facts are chosen and on whether the conclusion really comes from more than one of them."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
